.TH "FLOX\-GENERATIONS\-ROLLBACK" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-generations\-rollback \- switch to the previous live generation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general\-options>] generations rollback
     [\-d=<path> | \-r=<owner/name>]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Switch to the previous live generation of the environment.
.PP
Rolling back to the previous generation restores the environment\(cqs
manifest and lockfile to the state of the previous generation, sets it
as the live generation, and adds an entry to generation history.
.PP
The previously live generation isn\(cqt always N\-1.
If you\(cqve previously rolled back from generation 3 \-> 2 then rolling
back again will take you from generation 2 \-> 3.
Similarly if you\(cqve switched from generation 3 \-> 1 then rolling
back will take you from generation 1 \-> 3.
.PP
Generations don\(cqt always have a linear history.
If you create generation 2 by installing a package, rollback to
generation 1 and create generation 3 by installing another package, then
generation 3 won\(cqt contain the package from generation 2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]flox\-generations\-history(1)\f[B]\f[R] can be used to see
the relationships between generations.
